  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Mycetinis scorodonius, commonly known as vampire's bane or garlic mushroom, is a small delicate fungus in the Omphalotaceae family recognized for its distinctive pungent garlic odor. This woodland mushroom is found throughout New England and other temperate regions, often appearing in clusters on decaying wood and leaf litter.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Garlic Odor",
      "description": "The most distinctive feature is its strong, unmistakable garlic or onion-like smell when crushed or handled, which becomes more pronounced when dried.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size and Cap",
      "description": "Caps are typically 0.5-1.5 cm in diameter, whitish to pale gray, and delicate with a thin structure and slightly inrolled margins.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pale Gills",
      "description": "Features white to pale pink gills that are well-spaced and run down the thin, fragile stem, which may have a slightly bulbous base.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Vampire's bane thrives in moist, shaded woodlands and forest floors where it grows on dead hardwood, leaf litter, and mossy substrates, appearing from summer through fall in New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The common name 'vampire's bane' refers to folklore associating garlic with protection from vampires, and this mushroom's pungent garlic smell earned it historical reverence in European folk traditions as a protective charm.",
